@description REAPER MCP — Model Context Protocol interface (immersive/spatial audio)
@version 1.21.0
@author James Livingston
@link https://github.com/inseglet-audio/inseglet
@provides
  [darwin] reaper_mcp.dylib https://github.com/inseglet-audio/inseglet/releases/download/v$version/reaper_mcp.dylib
@changelog
  The true-peak interpolator is now our own design, and the estimator says all four of its bounds as numbers.

  WHAT CHANGES.

  The true-peak estimator replaces the ITU-R BS.1770-4 Annex 2 table (12 taps at 4x oversampling -- the
  Recommendation's minimum) with a 24-tap, 8x polyphase interpolator designed for this product. Measured
  the same way on the same EBU programme material, every window read in the context of its file against an
  exact reference: the worst under-read falls from 0.52 dB to 0.14 dB (SQAM 27, castanets) and the worst
  over-read from 0.22 dB to 0.10 dB (SQAM 64); the standard's own fs/4 test tones read 0.13 dB low, inside
  Tech 3341's window. `truePeakDb` MOVES on real material -- that is the point of the change.

  Every disclosed bound followed the table, because each is computed from it at first call rather than
  written down: `truePeakGridBoundDb` 0.169 dB (was 0.688), `truePeakFilterGainBoundDb` +0.086 dB (was
  +0.222), `truePeakEdgeGuardSamples` 12 (was 6; it is taps/2).

  TWO NEW FIELDS complete the disclosure in numbers, on every payload that carries the others:
  `truePeakFilterLossBoundDb` (0.088 dB) -- how far the interpolation filter itself can read a sine low
  within its passband, beyond what the grid misses; and `truePeakSineLowBoundDb` (0.127 dB, reached at a
  quarter of the sample rate) -- the estimator's actual worst under-read of a steady sine with the grid,
  the filter's loss and its phase error all in, tighter than the sum of the two. The passband edge ships
  as a constant (0.45 of the sample rate) beside the table.

  THE BOUNDS ARE FOR A SINE, and the description says so: for a sine inside the passband the interior
  reading is never lower than the truth minus `truePeakSineLowBoundDb` and never higher than the truth
  plus `truePeakFilterGainBoundDb`.

  COST. The meter now runs one interpolation pass for both true-peak numbers where it ran two. Measured on
  a machine a load witness certified quiet for the whole run, the new meter call takes 3.49x the previous
  one -- about 0.4 s per minute of audio at 48 kHz, one channel, where the previous meter spent 0.11 s.
